1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,088
This is ECE 358, video 17. The topics are NAT, autonomous systems, and the basics of routing.

2
00:00:09,538 --> 00:00:16,258
After this video, you can tell public and private IP addresses apart, and list the private blocks.

3
00:00:16,708 --> 00:00:21,103
You can fill a NAT translation table, and rewrite each datagram.

4
00:00:21,553 --> 00:00:26,310
You can explain an autonomous system, and name its routing protocols.

5
00:00:26,760 --> 00:00:31,965
You can model a router network as a graph, and classify routing algorithms.

6
00:00:32,415 --> 00:00:37,194
And you can answer question bank Network questions 68 to 80.

7
00:00:37,644 --> 00:00:50,359
Before we study NAT, we need two kinds of IP addresses. A public IP address is globally unique. Routers everywhere know how to reach it. Here the server has a public address.

8
00:00:50,809 --> 00:00:57,230
So any host on the Internet can open a TCP connection to a machine with a public address.

9
00:00:57,680 --> 00:01:11,205
A private IP address is unique only inside one organization. Here the hosts 10 dot 0 dot 0 dot 1 to 10 dot 0 dot 0 dot 3 have private addresses.

10
00:01:11,655 --> 00:01:25,245
Routers outside the organization do not know a private address, because it is not advertised outside. So an outside host cannot open a TCP connection to a host with a private address.

11
00:01:25,695 --> 00:01:39,860
Private addresses have two advantages. First, addresses are reused. For example, the University of Waterloo and another university can use the same private address. NAT makes this possible.

12
00:01:40,310 --> 00:01:48,310
Second, they add security. Someone outside cannot open a connection to an organization server with a private address.

13
00:01:48,760 --> 00:01:59,811
RFC 1918 reserves three blocks for private addresses. The first block is 10 dot 0 dot 0 dot 0, slash 8.

14
00:02:00,261 --> 00:02:08,879
Slash 8 means that the first 8 bits are fixed. The other 32 minus 8, which is 24 bits, are free host bits.

15
00:02:09,329 --> 00:02:20,764
Valid addresses go from 10 dot 0 dot 0 dot 1 to 10 dot 255 dot 255 dot 254.

16
00:02:21,214 --> 00:02:33,353
The second block is 172 dot 16 dot 0 dot 0, slash 12. 12 bits are fixed, and 32 minus 12, which is 20 bits, are free.

17
00:02:33,803 --> 00:02:54,091
Slash 12 fixes the first 4 bits of the second byte: 0 0 0 1. The last 4 bits are free. So the second byte goes from 0 0 0 1 0 0 0 0, which is 16, to 0 0 0 1 1 1 1 1, which is 31.

18
00:02:54,541 --> 00:03:07,341
So valid addresses go from 172 dot 16 dot 0 dot 1 to 172 dot 31 dot 255 dot 254.

19
00:03:07,791 --> 00:03:19,759
The third block is 192 dot 168 dot 0 dot 0, slash 16. 32 minus 16 leaves 16 free host bits.

20
00:03:20,209 --> 00:03:34,843
Valid addresses go from 192 dot 168 dot 0 dot 1 to 192 dot 168 dot 255 dot 254.

21
00:03:35,293 --> 00:03:45,363
Each range leaves out two addresses at the ends. All host bits 0 gives the network ID. All host bits 1 gives the broadcast address.

22
00:03:45,813 --> 00:03:59,914
The University of Waterloo uses 172 dot 16 slash 12, and 10 slash 8 for WiFi. RFC means Request For Comments, a document of the I E T F.

23
00:04:00,364 --> 00:04:18,689
NAT means network address translation. With NAT, all devices in a local network share one IPv4 address, as far as the outside world is concerned. Here the local network is 10 dot 0 dot 0 dot 0, slash 24.

24
00:04:19,139 --> 00:04:27,481
The NAT router has the public address 138 dot 76 dot 29 dot 7 on the Internet side.

25
00:04:27,931 --> 00:04:33,968
On the local side, the NAT router has the address 10 dot 0 dot 0 dot 4.

26
00:04:34,418 --> 00:04:49,138
Inside the local network, datagrams use the 10 dot 0 dot 0 addresses as usual. Here host 10 dot 0 dot 0 dot 1 sends from port 3345.

27
00:04:49,588 --> 00:04:59,145
Every datagram that leaves the local network has the same source address, 138 dot 76 dot 29 dot 7.

28
00:04:59,595 --> 00:05:06,849
But each one gets a different source port number: 5001, 5002 and 5003.

29
00:05:07,299 --> 00:05:16,216
So NAT maps the private addresses to one public address, with different layer 4 port numbers. The port number tells the hosts apart.

30
00:05:16,666 --> 00:05:31,898
All devices in the local network use private addresses from the three blocks. NAT has four advantages. First, the local network needs just one IP address from its provider ISP, for all devices.

31
00:05:32,348 --> 00:05:38,897
Second, the network can change the address of a local host without notifying the outside world.

32
00:05:39,347 --> 00:05:49,118
Third, the network can change its ISP without changing the addresses of the local devices. Only the one public address changes.

33
00:05:49,568 --> 00:05:57,931
Fourth, security. Devices inside the local network are not directly addressable or visible from the outside world.

34
00:05:58,381 --> 00:06:13,058
The NAT router does three jobs. Job 1 is for outgoing datagrams. A datagram from the local network has the source 10 dot 0 dot 0 dot 1, port 3345.

35
00:06:13,508 --> 00:06:29,380
The router replaces the source IP address and port with the NAT IP address, 138 dot 76 dot 29 dot 7, and a new port number, 5001. The rewritten field is orange.

36
00:06:29,830 --> 00:06:41,115
Job 2. The router remembers every translation pair in the NAT translation table: the NAT IP address and new port, and the source IP address and port.

37
00:06:41,565 --> 00:06:54,024
Job 3 is for incoming datagrams. A reply arrives with the destination 138 dot 76 dot 29 dot 7, port 5001.

38
00:06:54,474 --> 00:07:05,973
The router replaces the destination with the matching address and port from the table: 10 dot 0 dot 0 dot 1, port 3345.

39
00:07:06,423 --> 00:07:19,905
Remote clients and servers reply to the NAT IP address and the new port. They never see the private address. The router does all three jobs transparently. The hosts do not know that NAT is there.

40
00:07:20,355 --> 00:07:28,505
Here is the example from slide 70. Host 10 dot 0 dot 0 dot 1 is inside the local network.

41
00:07:28,955 --> 00:07:37,765
It wants to talk to a web server at 128 dot 119 dot 40 dot 186, port 80.

42
00:07:38,215 --> 00:07:45,042
The NAT router has the public address 138 dot 76 dot 29 dot 7.

43
00:07:45,492 --> 00:07:48,905
The NAT translation table is empty at the start.

44
00:07:49,355 --> 00:07:59,361
Step 1. The host sends a datagram. The source is 10 dot 0 dot 0 dot 1, port 3345.

45
00:07:59,811 --> 00:08:07,661
The destination is 128 dot 119 dot 40 dot 186, port 80.

46
00:08:08,111 --> 00:08:27,717
Step 2. The NAT router changes the source from 10 dot 0 dot 0 dot 1, port 3345, to 138 dot 76 dot 29 dot 7, port 5001. It writes this pair into the NAT translation table.

47
00:08:28,167 --> 00:08:31,601
Then the NAT router sends the datagram to the Internet.

48
00:08:32,051 --> 00:08:43,379
Step 3. The reply arrives from the server. Its source is 128 dot 119 dot 40 dot 186, port 80.

49
00:08:43,829 --> 00:08:51,595
Its destination is 138 dot 76 dot 29 dot 7, port 5001.

50
00:08:52,045 --> 00:09:08,749
Step 4. The router looks up 138 dot 76 dot 29 dot 7, port 5001 in the table. The table gives 10 dot 0 dot 0 dot 1, port 3345.

51
00:09:09,199 --> 00:09:14,724
The router writes this into the destination fields, and the datagram reaches the host.

52
00:09:15,174 --> 00:09:21,510
Only the address and port of the inside host change. The server never sees the private address.

53
00:09:21,960 --> 00:09:30,067
Some people do not like NAT. First, a router should process a datagram only up to layer 3, the network layer.

54
00:09:30,517 --> 00:09:43,082
But a NAT router also changes the port number, which is in the layer 4 header. This violates the end-to-end argument, because a network-layer device changes port numbers.

55
00:09:43,532 --> 00:09:48,652
Second, some say that IPv6 should solve the address shortage, not NAT.

56
00:09:49,102 --> 00:09:57,379
Third, there is the NAT traversal problem: a client that wants to connect to a server behind NAT. The class skips this part.

57
00:09:57,829 --> 00:10:06,960
But NAT is here to stay. Home networks, institutional networks, and 4G and 5G cellular networks use it extensively.

58
00:10:07,410 --> 00:10:18,183
An autonomous system, or AS, is a set of routers under a single technical administration. One organization owns and manages everything in it.

59
00:10:18,633 --> 00:10:27,124
Inside the AS, the routers use an interior gateway protocol, with common metrics, to route packets within the AS.

60
00:10:27,574 --> 00:10:36,001
To route packets to other ASes, the AS uses an exterior gateway protocol. The red routers at the edges do this.

61
00:10:36,451 --> 00:10:43,640
Each AS has an identifier called the AS number. It was 16 bits. Now it is 32 bits.

62
00:10:44,090 --> 00:10:57,573
Slide 74 is an extra slide. It shows the Internet as a set of autonomous systems. Each pink area is one AS. Inside each AS are ordinary internal routers.

63
00:10:58,023 --> 00:11:05,255
The blue routers are BGP routers, at the borders of the ASes. They connect the ASes to each other.

64
00:11:05,705 --> 00:11:18,654
BGP routers talk to each other over logical connections, which are TCP connections. So the Internet has two levels: routing inside each AS, and routing among ASes.

65
00:11:19,104 --> 00:11:29,408
Slide 75 is an extra slide about the University of Waterloo. UW is autonomous system number 12093.

66
00:11:29,858 --> 00:11:42,423
UW connects to three other ASes. The first is ORION, the Ontario Research and Innovation Optical Network, AS 26677.

67
00:11:42,873 --> 00:11:48,164
The second is Beanfield Tech, AS 21949.

68
00:11:48,614 --> 00:11:53,137
The third is Rogers Communications, AS 812.

69
00:11:53,587 --> 00:12:11,464
UW advertises three prefixes. Two are IPv4: 129 dot 97 dot 0 dot 0, slash 16, and 129 dot 97 dot 248 dot 0, slash 21.

70
00:12:11,914 --> 00:12:15,925
The third is one slash 47 prefix for IPv6.

71
00:12:16,375 --> 00:12:21,879
The number of BGP routers in the UW AS is not publicly known.

72
00:12:22,329 --> 00:12:33,507
Routing protocols build the routing tables. There are two groups. Intra-AS routing works within one AS. Its protocols are interior gateway protocols.

73
00:12:33,957 --> 00:12:40,208
The first is OSPF, Open Shortest Path First. It is a link-state protocol.

74
00:12:40,658 --> 00:12:47,549
Or the AS can use RIP, the Routing Information Protocol. It is a distance vector protocol.

75
00:12:47,999 --> 00:12:55,103
Or E I G R P, the Enhanced Interior Gateway Routing Protocol. It came from Cisco.

76
00:12:55,553 --> 00:13:01,654
Inter-AS routing works among ASes. Its protocols are exterior gateway protocols.

77
00:13:02,104 --> 00:13:09,613
The Internet uses only one: BGP, the Border Gateway Protocol. There is no second protocol.

78
00:13:10,063 --> 00:13:22,437
Slide 77 is an extra slide. It shows where the routing protocols run inside a router. OSPF runs directly on top of IP, in the network layer.

79
00:13:22,887 --> 00:13:27,793
Next to it is ICMP, the Internet Control Message Protocol.

80
00:13:28,243 --> 00:13:32,766
RIP is an application layer protocol that uses UDP.

81
00:13:33,216 --> 00:13:38,400
BGP is an application layer protocol that uses TCP.

82
00:13:38,850 --> 00:13:45,015
All of these routing protocols read, write, and delete entries in the routing tables.

83
00:13:45,465 --> 00:13:49,156
The system administrator can also configure entries.

84
00:13:49,606 --> 00:13:56,689
Forwarding then uses the routing tables to send each arriving IP packet out on the correct link.

85
00:13:57,139 --> 00:14:09,491
Now we move to routing protocols. The goal of a routing protocol is to find good paths, also called routes, from sending hosts to receiving hosts, through the network of routers.

86
00:14:09,941 --> 00:14:22,549
A path is the sequence of routers that packets traverse from the initial source host to the final destination host. The green line is one path, from u through x and y to z.

87
00:14:22,999 --> 00:14:26,583
Good means least cost, fastest, or least congested.

88
00:14:27,033 --> 00:14:32,771
Routing is one of the top 10 challenges in networking. It still has research interest.

89
00:14:33,221 --> 00:14:40,304
To design routing algorithms, we model the network of routers as a graph, G equals N comma E.

90
00:14:40,754 --> 00:14:46,557
N is the set of nodes, which are the routers: u, v, w, x, y, and z.

91
00:14:47,007 --> 00:14:51,060
E is the set of edges, which are the links between the routers.

92
00:14:51,510 --> 00:14:57,035
Each link has a cost. c a b is the cost of the direct link between a and b.

93
00:14:57,485 --> 00:15:03,459
For example, the link from w to z has cost 5. So c w z is 5.

94
00:15:03,909 --> 00:15:08,261
u and z have no direct link. So c u z is infinity.

95
00:15:08,711 --> 00:15:17,841
The network operator defines the cost. It could always be 1. Or it could be inversely related to bandwidth, or to congestion.

96
00:15:18,291 --> 00:15:32,670
One detail. The figure shows 10 links, including the cost 5 link from u to w on top. The edge list on the slide has only 9. It leaves out u w. This video uses the figure.

97
00:15:33,120 --> 00:15:50,421
Routing algorithms are classified on two axes. The first axis asks: global or decentralized information? In a global algorithm, all routers have the complete topology and all link costs. These are link state algorithms.

98
00:15:50,871 --> 00:16:06,957
In a decentralized algorithm, the computation is iterative, with exchange of information with neighbors. At first, each router knows only the costs of the links to its attached neighbors. These are distance vector algorithms.

99
00:16:07,407 --> 00:16:15,087
The second axis asks: how fast do routes change? In a static algorithm, routes change slowly over time.

100
00:16:15,537 --> 00:16:23,686
In a dynamic algorithm, routes change more quickly, with periodic updates or in response to link cost changes.

101
00:16:24,136 --> 00:16:31,944
The next videos teach the two main classes: link state, with Dijkstra and OSPF, and then distance vector.

102
00:16:32,394 --> 00:16:42,954
Now we solve question bank Network questions 68 to 80. Each question appears first. Pause the video and try it. Then compare with the solution.

103
00:16:43,404 --> 00:16:48,951
The question bank has no published answers. These solutions come from the slides only.

104
00:16:49,401 --> 00:16:59,001
Question 68. Identify the key differences between public IP addresses and private IP addresses. Pause the video and try it.

105
00:16:59,451 --> 00:17:08,475
From slide 65. A public address is globally unique. A private address is unique only inside the organization.

106
00:17:08,925 --> 00:17:19,079
Routers everywhere recognize a public address. Routers outside the organization do not recognize a private address, because it is not advertised.

107
00:17:19,529 --> 00:17:29,172
Any host can open a TCP connection to a public address. An outside host cannot open a TCP connection to a private address.

108
00:17:29,622 --> 00:17:37,857
Private addresses come from the three RFC 1918 blocks. Public addresses are outside these blocks.

109
00:17:38,307 --> 00:17:48,909
Question 69. What are the advantages for an enterprise that uses a combination of public and private IP addresses? Pause the video and try it.

110
00:17:49,359 --> 00:18:03,503
From slides 65 and 68. First, reuse of addresses. Private addresses can be reused in many organizations with NAT. So the enterprise needs only a few public addresses.

111
00:18:03,953 --> 00:18:16,348
Second, security. Servers with private addresses cannot receive a TCP connection from outside. The enterprise keeps public addresses for the machines that must be reachable.

112
00:18:16,798 --> 00:18:23,262
Question 70. Give two examples of private IP addresses. Pause the video and try it.

113
00:18:23,712 --> 00:18:35,147
Take any valid address from the three blocks. For example, 10 dot 0 dot 0 dot 1, from the block 10 dot 0 dot 0 dot 0, slash 8.

114
00:18:35,597 --> 00:18:53,325
And 192 dot 168 dot 1 dot 5, from the block 192 dot 168 dot 0 dot 0, slash 16. 172 dot 16 dot 0 dot 1 is also correct.

115
00:18:53,775 --> 00:19:10,351
Question 71. Two routers, R1 and R2, are directly connected with one physical link. How many interfaces are in the subnet? What is the prefix length? Are there subnets with a smaller prefix length? Pause the video and try it.

116
00:19:10,801 --> 00:19:17,265
Part a. A subnet is the set of interfaces that can reach each other without a router in between.

117
00:19:17,715 --> 00:19:28,552
This link has one interface on R1 and one on R2. So the subnet has 2 interfaces. The addresses here are an example. The question gives none.

118
00:19:29,002 --> 00:19:32,714
Part b. We need 2 addresses for the 2 interfaces.

119
00:19:33,164 --> 00:19:39,329
We also need the network ID and the broadcast address. 2 plus 2 is 4 addresses.

120
00:19:39,779 --> 00:19:43,833
4 is 2 to the power 2. So the host part has 2 bits.

121
00:19:44,283 --> 00:19:50,021
The prefix length is 32 minus 2, which is 30. So the answer is slash 30.

122
00:19:50,471 --> 00:20:02,461
Part c. Yes. A smaller prefix length gives a larger block. Slash 29 has 3 host bits. 2 to the power 3, minus 2, is 6 usable addresses.

123
00:20:02,911 --> 00:20:10,313
Slash 28 has 4 host bits. 2 to the power 4, minus 2, is 14 usable addresses.

124
00:20:10,763 --> 00:20:18,913
These blocks also hold the 2 interfaces, but they waste addresses. Slash 30 is the longest prefix that works.

125
00:20:19,363 --> 00:20:32,547
Questions 72 and 73. What is the main function of the NAT protocol? And state the NAT protocol in terms of input and output IP packets. Pause the video and try it.

126
00:20:32,997 --> 00:20:47,866
Question 72. The main function of NAT is to let all devices in a local network share one public IPv4 address. NAT maps their private addresses to that one address, with different port numbers.

127
00:20:48,316 --> 00:21:01,052
Question 73. For an outgoing packet, the input has a private source address and port: 10 dot 0 dot 0 dot 1, port 3345.

128
00:21:01,502 --> 00:21:16,030
The output has the NAT IP address and a new port as the source: 138 dot 76 dot 29 dot 7, port 5001. The router stores this pair in the NAT table.

129
00:21:16,480 --> 00:21:22,987
For an incoming packet, the input has the NAT IP address and the new port as the destination.

130
00:21:23,437 --> 00:21:29,879
The router looks up the pair. The output has the stored private address and port as the destination.

131
00:21:30,329 --> 00:21:35,215
All other fields stay the same. This answer comes from slide 69.

132
00:21:35,665 --> 00:21:47,974
Questions 74 and 75. Recall the four types of packet delay. Does NAT have an impact on packet delay? If yes, which component? Pause the video and try it.

133
00:21:48,424 --> 00:21:54,973
Question 74. There are four delays at each node. d proc is the processing delay.

134
00:21:55,423 --> 00:21:59,306
d queue is the queueing delay, the wait in the output buffer.

135
00:21:59,756 --> 00:22:03,319
d trans is the transmission delay, L over R.

136
00:22:03,769 --> 00:22:07,246
d prop is the propagation delay, d over s.

137
00:22:07,696 --> 00:22:12,752
Question 75. Yes. NAT adds to d proc at the NAT router.

138
00:22:13,202 --> 00:22:22,354
For every datagram, the router must look up the NAT table and rewrite the IP address and port number. Then the checksums must change too.

139
00:22:22,804 --> 00:22:35,817
This answer is reasoning from slide 69 and Chapter 1. No single slide states it. d trans and d prop do not change, because the length of the datagram and the link stay the same.

140
00:22:36,267 --> 00:22:49,537
Questions 76 and 77. What is an autonomous system? Name one intra-AS routing protocol and one inter-AS routing protocol. Pause the video and try it.

141
00:22:49,987 --> 00:22:59,075
Question 76, from slide 73. An autonomous system is a set of routers under a single technical administration.

142
00:22:59,525 --> 00:23:09,893
It uses an interior gateway protocol to route packets inside the AS, and an exterior gateway protocol to route packets to other ASes.

143
00:23:10,343 --> 00:23:12,028
It has an AS number.

144
00:23:12,478 --> 00:23:18,686
Question 77. For intra-AS routing, OSPF. RIP is also correct.

145
00:23:19,136 --> 00:23:21,696
For inter-AS routing, BGP.

146
00:23:22,146 --> 00:23:31,639
Questions 78 to 80. What is the goal of routing protocols? What is a path? What is a good path? Pause the video and try it.

147
00:23:32,089 --> 00:23:47,663
Question 78, from slide 78. The goal is to determine good paths, or routes, from sending hosts to receiving hosts, through the network of routers. The routing protocols put the results into the routing tables.

148
00:23:48,113 --> 00:24:01,617
Question 79. A path is the sequence of routers that packets traverse from the initial source host to the final destination host. The green line is the path u, x, y, z.

149
00:24:02,067 --> 00:24:10,771
Question 80. A good path has the least cost, or is the fastest, or is the least congested. The operator sets the cost.

150
00:24:11,221 --> 00:24:17,045
In the graph, the path u, x, y, z costs 1 plus 1 plus 2, which is 4.

151
00:24:17,495 --> 00:24:27,137
The direct path u, w, z costs 5 plus 5, which is 10. So u, x, y, z is the better path. It has the least cost.

152
00:24:27,587 --> 00:24:33,433
To recap. Private addresses come from three blocks, and they are not advertised outside.

153
00:24:33,883 --> 00:24:41,883
A NAT router rewrites the source of outgoing datagrams, stores the pair, and maps incoming destinations back.

154
00:24:42,333 --> 00:24:46,663
NAT adds processing delay, and it changes a layer 4 field.

155
00:24:47,113 --> 00:24:59,999
An autonomous system is a set of routers under one administration. OSPF, RIP, or E I G R P route inside an AS. BGP routes among ASes.

156
00:25:00,449 --> 00:25:07,467
Routing algorithms work on a graph with link costs. The cost is infinity when there is no direct link.

157
00:25:07,917 --> 00:25:18,755
Global algorithms are link state, and decentralized algorithms are distance vector. The next video teaches Dijkstra's algorithm and OSPF.
